        "block-1": {
          "type": "content_block",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "The 4 C's of Diamonds",
            "body": "<p>When it comes to evaluating a diamond's quality, the 4 C's — Cut, Colour, Clarity, and Carat Weight — are essential factors to consider.</p>",
            "callout": false
          }
        },
        "block-2": {
          "type": "content_block",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Colour",
            "body": "<p>Diamonds come in various shades, ranging from colourless to fancy vivid colours. The less colour a diamond has, the higher its value. The GIA colour grading scale begins with D, representing colourless, and progresses through to Z, representing diamonds with noticeable colour.</p>",
            "callout": false,
            "list_items": "<strong>Colourless (D–F):</strong> highly valued for rarity and icy white appearance.\n<strong>Near Colourless (G–J):</strong> slight traces of colour, usually difficult to detect when mounted.\n<strong>Faint Yellow (K–M):</strong> a faint hue that becomes more noticeable down the scale.\n<strong>Very Light Yellow (N–R):</strong> noticeable yellow or brown tint.\n<strong>Light Yellow (S–Z):</strong> more pronounced colouration that can affect appearance and value.",
            "list_style": "numbered",
            "image_1_alt": "Diamond colour grading chart",
            "image_1_fallback": "diamond-colour-grades-chart.webp"
          }
        },
        "block-3": {
          "type": "content_block",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Carat Weight",
            "body": "<p>Carat weight is a measure of a diamond's size, with larger diamonds typically being more valuable. Price can change significantly at popular benchmark weights such as 0.50, 1.00 and 2.00 carats.</p><p>When choosing a diamond, balance carat weight with cut, colour and clarity to find the stone that best suits your preferences and budget.</p>",
            "callout": true,
            "image_1_alt": "Round brilliant cut diamond illustration",
            "image_1_fallback": "cameron-co-diamond-brilliant-cut-illustration.webp"
          }
        },
        "block-3b": {
          "type": "content_block",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Diamond Shapes",
            "body": "<p>Shape is a matter of personal style rather than quality — it doesn't appear on a grading certificate alongside the 4 C's, but it does affect how a diamond wears and how large it looks on the hand.</p>",
            "callout": false,
            "image_1_alt": "Ten diamond shapes: round, oval, princess, cushion, emerald, pear, marquise, heart, radiant and Asscher",
            "image_1_fallback": "diamond-shapes-chart.webp"
          }
        },
        "block-4": {
          "type": "content_block",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Cut",
            "body": "<p>Diamond cut refers to the way a diamond is shaped and faceted to enhance brilliance, fire, and overall appearance. A well-cut diamond can maximise its optical properties, whereas a poorly cut diamond may appear dull.</p>",
            "callout": false,
            "list_items": "<strong>Proportions:</strong> depth, table size, crown angle and pavilion angle affect how light travels within the stone.\n<strong>Symmetry:</strong> balanced facets help distribute light throughout the diamond.\n<strong>Polish:</strong> surface quality can affect clarity and brilliance.\n<strong>Cut Grade:</strong> gemmological laboratories assign grades from Excellent to Poor.",
            "list_style": "bullet",
            "image_1_alt": "Anatomy of a diamond",
            "image_1_fallback": "anatomy-of-a-diamond-chart.webp"
          }
        },
        "block-5": {
          "type": "content_block",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Clarity",
            "body": "<p>Clarity refers to the presence of internal inclusions and external blemishes. The fewer inclusions, the higher the clarity grade. The GIA clarity scale ranges from Flawless to Included.</p>",
            "callout": false,
            "list_items": "<strong>Flawless (FL):</strong> no inclusions or blemishes visible under 10x magnification.\n<strong>Internally Flawless (IF):</strong> no inclusions visible under 10x magnification.\n<strong>Very, Very Slightly Included (VVS1 and VVS2):</strong> inclusions are extremely difficult to see.\n<strong>Very Slightly Included (VS1 and VS2):</strong> minor inclusions visible under magnification but usually not to the naked eye.\n<strong>Slightly Included (SI1 and SI2):</strong> inclusions noticeable under magnification and sometimes visible.\n<strong>Included (I1, I2, I3):</strong> inclusions can affect transparency and brilliance.",
            "list_style": "numbered",
            "cta_text": "Book Appointment",
            "cta_link": "/pages/booking"
          }
        },
